{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-341612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌊x⌋\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)=u(x-2),\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌊x⌋$ is the floor function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,2,3,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/084c469b225d2a4cc4bc148fa3a42fb1501141d1adcd91d8d37999409a8d313f.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the point $x=2,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341612" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56eb45eed26fea4ae49ea290074778b9da5eeee6347b1f98ed258091a829b772"} {"question" "q-341612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341612" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ and $g$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd},\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{5}{2})+u(x-4).\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/d5a497337426dad07096702a3e211f525a959add8cb1fbaaef05fccac26d634b.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=1,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ and $x=4,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to 5/{2}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,g,$ and $h.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56eb45eed26fea4ae49ea290074778b9da5eeee6347b1f98ed258091a829b772"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$f,g,$ and $h$"] ["text" "$g$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341642" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f,g,$ and $h$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/assets/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56eb45eed26fea4ae49ea290074778b9da5eeee6347b1f98ed258091a829b772"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$f,g$ and $h$"] ["text" "$g$ only"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-341645" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}},\\,h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-3, & x<0 \\\\ -2x+5, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0$ into the two open intervals $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1).$ For $x\\ne 0,$\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{3/5},\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{5{x}^{2/5}}.\n$$\n\nWhile this derivative is continuous on the open pieces $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the limits of the derivative as $x\\to 0$ are not finite. In fact, the derivative becomes unbounded near $x=0.$ Thus, the condition for finite limits at the endpoints is not met.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{10/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{7/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $h$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56eb45eed26fea4ae49ea290074778b9da5eeee6347b1f98ed258091a829b772"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f$ and $g$ only"] ["text" "$f$ and $h$ only"] ["text" "$f$ only"] ["text" "$f,$ $g,$ and $h$"] ["text" "$h$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f$ and $h$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions are piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1]?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,,\\,g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}},\\,h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is **piecewise smooth** on $x\\in [a,b]$ if\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous on $[a,b],$ and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists and is continuous except possibly at finitely many points, where its one-sided limits exist and are finite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ We split the interval at $x=0.$ On each open interval, $(-1,0)$ and $(0,1),$ the function is linear. On these intervals,\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4x-1, & x<0 \\\\ 4x+3, & x>0\\end{aligned}\\,,\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x<0 \\\\ 4, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ are piecewise continuous on each open interval, and they both have finite limits at the endpoints of the subintervals.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\ng(x)={x}^{2}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{2}+{x}^{4/3},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)=2x+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{1/3}.\n$$\n\nHere, $g(x)$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $g(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in [-1,1].$ Since\n\n$$\nh(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}={x}^{14/5},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}{x}^{9/5}.\n$$\n\nHere, $h(x)$ and ${h}^{′}(x)$ are continuous on $[-1,1].$ Thus, $h(x)$ is continuously differentiable on the whole interval, and hence piecewise smooth.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f,$ $g,$ and $h.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/state.json" "file_sha256" "759f0dd62d365d28467414a7726b362b472d18a5e5aac3c9d639d42c9ec6624c"} {"question" "q-394140" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064583/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "56eb45eed26fea4ae49ea290074778b9da5eeee6347b1f98ed258091a829b772"} {"question" "q-39414